\details{
\subsection{Groups}{

The allocation is done group by group. A group is a pair of conditions, one
from \code{condition_alloc_st_list} and one from \code{condition_matched_data_list}:
the first selects the catch rows that are allocated together, the second the
catch rows whose sampled distributions are pooled to estimate the
distribution of those that have none. The two lists have the same length and
the same names - a group's name, \code{GA1}, \code{GA2}, ... when the lists have none -
and each condition is written as in \code{dplyr::filter()}: a quosure
(\code{rlang::quo(metier4 == "TBB")}), a text that is turned into one, or a
logical vector with a value for every row of \code{catch_data}.

The allocation strata must not overlap - a catch row in two of them is an
error, which \code{\link[=check_group_conditions]{check_group_conditions()}} reports before anything is
allocated. The matched data may overlap, and usually do: a group can draw
on a wider pool than its own strata. Every group is allocated within one
year and one stock.
}

\subsection{How a distribution is estimated}{

The numbers at length (or age) of a stratum without a sampled distribution
are the pooled sampled numbers of the matched data, scaled by the weight of
the stratum's catch over the weight of the catch that was sampled. Its mean
weight at length (or age) is the mean of the sampled mean weights, weighted
by the numbers at that class (\code{weighting = "NumberAtAoL"}) or by the catch
(\code{"CATON"}). A stratum that has a sampled distribution keeps it as it is.

Catch and distributions are linked on \code{vesselFlagCountry}, \code{year},
\code{workingGroup}, \code{stock}, \code{speciesCode}, \code{catchCategory} and
\code{domainBiology}. Units are harmonised first - catch weight to kg and
thousands, distribution values to g and numbers (N) - so what comes back is
in those.
}

\subsection{What comes back}{

A list of two tables:
\itemize{
\item \code{distribution}: every distribution row - the sampled ones as they came, and
the estimated ones - with \code{sampledOrEstimated} (\code{"sampled"} or
\code{"estimated"}) and \code{allocGroup}, the group they were allocated in.
Numbers are \code{variableType == "Number"} rows, mean weights
\code{"WeightLive"} rows.
\item \code{catch}: the allocated catch rows, with \code{allocGroup}. A stratum without a
\code{domainBiology} is given one (\code{"[alloc]_<season>_<area>_<unit>_<fleet>_<category>"}),
and its estimated distributions carry it, so the two tables join on
\code{domainBiology} - which is why anything that reads an allocation takes both
tables from this result, not the catch that went in.
}

With \code{assembled_output = TRUE} the rows that were not allocated (another
origin or variable type, or in no group) are added, with \code{allocGroup} \code{NA}.
